Z||000000||FIERO1985||01-22-2001||08:01 PM||||I have a 2.8 that is modified but has a large amount of Back pressure Is there steps to lower this and If so what are they??<P><BR>Thanks <P>------------------<BR>1985 GT V6 <BR>"LETS RACE"||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||01-22-2001||08:16 PM||||how do you know there is a back pressure problem? have you actually measured it? Measuring will tell you exactly where the problem is if there is one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIERO1985||01-22-2001||08:25 PM||||what do you mean to measure the backpressure when accelerating I then let off gas to shift or slow down there is a bubbling back pressure and I just wanted to know how to minimize this.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>1985 GT V6 <BR>"LETS RACE"||||reg||1
Z||000003||hugh||01-22-2001||09:00 PM||||It sounds like a rich fuel mixture.When all cars were carbed it was common for the exhaust to make a bubbling or burble(for want of a more descriptive word)sound when your foot came off the gas pedal.When you lift off the gas the pressure in the exhaust drops,but if the mixture is rich you may have some combustion in the exhaust system.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-23-2001||12:00 AM||||that's not back pressure. It's the exhaust "talking." It's pretty normal. In EFI motors it's pretty harmless. (In carb motors is can blow the exhaust out.)<P>This happens mainly in stick cars but automatics can do it to. it's caused when the engine is trying to suck more air than the throttle position will give it.<P>The condition is similar to a truck "jake brake." The truck does it a diferant way but the end result is called engine braking. It's a normal behavior and actually a desirable effect. (Diesle vehicles without the "jake brake" tend to eat brake parts quite fast. Especially when towing or hauling somehting heavy.)<P>When the throttle closes and the stick car is in gear, intake vacuum goes thru the roof. If you hook up a vac gauge you can see it happen. Vac in the high 20's isn't uncommon for short periods.<P>In a car with a carb the mix would go really rich because the high engine vacuum under this condition would suck fuel from the idle feeds. (It's not uncommon to shoot flames from short exhausts.) EFI doesn't tend to go as rich but will still talk from the braking effect.<P>If you are hearing it loudly then check for exhaust leaks. Other wise you may want to see about a new/differeant muffler if it bothers you that much.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||01-23-2001||04:52 AM||||My 2.8 burbles alot.. I think it sounds pretty darn cool  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Get flames between shifts, too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||80's BOY||01-23-2001||08:24 AM||||i think pontiac did a good job with the amount of backpressure on the factory exhaust. when i replaced my 85 gt's exhaust with a freeflow setup and resonated tips, i lost all low end torque. i always wondered if i took out the glasspack and replaced it with a flowmaster if i would have better results. <P><BR>...well, not ALL low end torque....but alot.<p>[This message has been edited by 80's BOY (edited 01-23-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000007||1FST2M6||01-23-2001||08:54 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6headerdyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6headerdyno.jpg</A> <P>this is what happens where there isn't enought back pressure you loose all the balls the car had and you kick your self..  but at least you've got shiney new parts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (before and after header install on a 2.8)  you got "girgling back pressure" uum i don't think that what your hearing.. that compression braking.. when you let off the accelerator and your coasting to a stop you should head some poping and such.. that unburn fuel in the cat cooking and the engine doing it job of slowing you down. <P>EASY way to see if there too much back pressure...   are your manifolds glowing red?  if they are you've got too much back pressure! Todd green had that problem and i did too with the cat that WAS on my car 1 7/8" unit that was old.. i installed a 2.5" to match the rest of the exhaust and POOF.. not more heat issues and got GREAT HP AND TQ numbers out of it! ||||reg||1
